 going to  demonstrate what a parasite  I'm just gonna do a picture of a dog and  we are going to think of an example of a  parasite because an example of a  parasite is a flea which is on the body  of a dog  so again apologies for my not-so-great  drawing of a dog there we go now in a  parasitic relationship there are two  different organisms one in this context  the dog is the host so living off the  host is a parasite in this context it's  a flea so there's a little flea which is  drinking the blood of the dog now in  this context the flea is the parasite  now in a parasitic relationship between  two organisms it's only beneficial for  one of the organisms it's only  beneficial for the parasite so parasitic  relationships is only good for one of  the two organisms in organisms involved  now the other type of relationship we  need to know about apart from parasitism  is mutualism and a really nice example  of a mutualistic relationship is the  relationship between plants and bees so  just take one second to draw these  plants and then over here is going to be  my B now unlike a parasitic relationship  where it's only good it's only  beneficial for one of the organisms in a  mutualistic relationship it's good for  both of the organisms so in this  particular example the benefit that the  bees get is nectar I Eve food from the  plants so as the beads go onto the  plants they get their food and then they  go to the next plant the benefit for the  plants is pollen basically stays on the  on the B on its legs and on it's on it's  on  and when it goes to the other plans it  transfers the pollen allowing the plants  to reproduce so the big difference  between a parasitic relationship and a  mutualistic relationship is a minute  mutualistic relationship is beneficial  for both organisms involved not just one  so that guy's shows interdependence  it shows how organisms affect have an  effect on each other and that effect on  each other and that relationship between  each o